    },
    {
      "block_id": 11,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The pitch of the sound produced by the waveform depends upon its frequency. To tell the system \nwhat frequency to use, you need to specify the sampling period. The sampling period specifies \nthe number of system clock ticks, or timing intervals, that should elapse between each sample \n(byte of audio data) fed to the digital-to-analog converter in the audio channel. There is a period \nregister for each audio channel. The value of the period register is used for count-down purposes; \neach time the register counts down to 0, another sample is retrieved from the waveform data set \nfor output. In units, the period value represents clock ticks per sample. The minimum period \nvalue you should use is 124 ticks per sample NTSC (123 PAL) and the maximum is 65535. \nThese limits apply to both PAL and NTSC machines. For high-quality sound, there are other \nconstraints on the sampling period (see the section called “ Producing High-quality Sound” ).\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.51097106933594,
        442.6959228515625,
        517.248046875,
        569.3289794921875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
